  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 320 AM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nGuidance initially places the Tri-State area in zonal flow Friday as \nwe position between a ridge to the west over the Rockies and a \ntrough to the east over the Great Lakes. The ridge really starts to \ntake form Saturday into Sunday. Southwest upper-level flow should \naccompany this setup with strong high pressure at the surface. The \nupper pattern is forecasted to weaken a bit Monday as a new system \nswings in from the west. This briefly ushers in westerly flow back \nto the County Warning Area (CWA) with weak low pressure at the \nsurface. Surface analysis currently shows no cold fronts or \nother frontal boundaries moving through the area at this time. A\nwarming trend is likely to influence the coverage area Friday \nthrough next Monday as high pressure and southwesterly flow \ndominate.\n\nIt will likely be a dry period for us as any precipitation would be \nlimited to shortwave passages. Thus, probability of precipitation \n(PoPs) are less than 5% the entire long-term forecast period due to \nlack of sufficient moisture. As mentioned above, warmer \ntemperatures are likely heading into next weekend. Friday's \nhighs in the upper 50s should warm up to the mid 60/low 70s \nSaturday. Sunday/Monday's highs are fixing to reach the \nmid/upper 70's. It will be seasonably pleasant with dew points \nin the 20s and 30s, moistening up to the 40s by next Monday.\n\nCritical fire weather is not expected to be a huge concern heading \ninto the weekend but will absolutely need to be monitored as this \nwork week goes on, nonetheless. Relative Humidity (RH) values are \nexpected to bounce around the 20s and 30s for the most part. The \ndays to keep an eye on will be Saturday and Sunday as RH may dip \ninto the teens. Surface winds Saturday will be from the southwest \ngenerally around 10-25 mph, gusting to 35 mph. Sunday's winds will \nsit around 5-15 mph, shifting from the southwest to northeast and \npotentially gusting up to 20 mph. Other than that, the rest of the \nforecast period should see a general 5-20 mph range of winds. Fire \nweather concerns will be mainly focused in our eastern Colorado \ncounties, west of US-385 where stronger wind gusts line up with \nlower RH.",
